/// The Surrealism extension interface defines the contract between a SurrealDB
/// host and a guest WASM component (an extension). All structured data crossing the boundary is
/// serialized with FlatBuffers; the `serialized-*` type aliases carry those opaque
/// byte buffers.
package surrealism:extension;

/// Shared serialization types used by both host and guest.
///
/// Every `serialized-*` alias wraps a FlatBuffers-encoded byte buffer.
/// The schemas live in `surrealdb/types/src/flatbuffers/` and must stay
/// in sync between host and guest SDK versions.
interface types {
    /// A single SurrealDB value (any type: record, object, array, scalar, etc.)
    /// encoded as a FlatBuffers byte buffer.
    type serialized-value = list<u8>;

    /// Query bind variables encoded as a FlatBuffers byte buffer.
    /// Represents a map of variable names to SurrealDB values.
    type serialized-vars = list<u8>;

    /// Ordered function arguments encoded as a FlatBuffers byte buffer.
    type serialized-args = list<u8>;

    /// A SurrealDB type descriptor (kind) encoded as a FlatBuffers byte buffer.
    /// Used to describe the return type of a function.
    type serialized-kind = list<u8>;

    /// Named function arguments encoded as a FlatBuffers `ArgumentList` byte buffer.
    /// Each entry pairs an argument name with its SurrealDB type descriptor (`Kind`).
    type serialized-arguments = list<u8>;
}

/// Host-provided capabilities imported by the guest module.
///
/// Every host call returns `result<T, host-error>`, whose variant says what
/// kind of failure occurred: a capability denial, a malformed request, a
/// datastore refusal, a deadline, or anything else.
interface host {
    use types.{serialized-value, serialized-vars, serialized-args};

    /// Why a host call failed.
    ///
    /// Classified so the host can count and alert on capability denials
    /// separately from an extension's own errors, and so a guest can match on the
    /// class rather than parsing a message. A denied call never runs.
    variant host-error {
        /// The module's declared capabilities forbid this call.
        permission-denied(string),
        /// The query or function name did not parse or resolve.
        invalid-request(string),
        /// The datastore refused or failed the operation. A write attempted
        /// through a read-only transaction arrives here.
        datastore(string),
        /// The host's deadline elapsed while serving this call.
        deadline-exceeded,
        /// Anything else, including serialization failures.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A borrowed capability to act inside the calling query's transaction.
    ///
    /// The host owns every handle and revokes it when `invoke` returns, so a
    /// guest cannot reach the datastore outside the invocation it was lent to.
    /// Methods carry the caller's auth, the caller's cursor document, and the
    /// module's capability narrowing.
    resource transaction {
        /// Execute a SurrealQL query string against the host datastore.
        ///
        /// The query runs with the permissions of the module's configured auth
        /// scope. `vars` supplies bind variables (pass an empty buffer for no
        /// variables). Returns the query result as a serialized value.
        sql: async func(query: string, vars: serialized-vars) -> result<serialized-value, host-error>;

        /// Call another surrealism function by fully-qualified name (e.g. `"mymod::greet"`).
        ///
        /// `version` optionally pins a specific module version; `none` resolves
        /// the latest. Cross-module calls are subject to the caller's grants.
        run: async func(fnc: string, version: option<string>, args: serialized-args) -> result<serialized-value, host-error>;
    }
}

/// The `extension` world is the top-level contract that every surrealism guest
/// module must satisfy.
///
/// ## Lifecycle
///
/// 1. `surreal ext build` calls `init` once, snapshots the memory it leaves
///    as the component's initial state, and removes the `init` export. The
///    module may use it to set up state or warm caches from its attached files.
/// 2. The host instantiates the WASM component afresh for every invocation, and
///    each instance starts in that state. The host never calls `init`: it
///    refuses a component that still exports it, which was not built by
///    `surreal ext build`.
/// 3. For each incoming request the host reads the function's entry in the
///    baked `exports.toml`, lends a matching transaction, and calls `invoke`.
///    The handle is revoked when the call returns.
///
/// ## Introspection
///
/// `list-functions`, `function-args`, and `function-returns` allow the host
/// (and tooling such as `surreal ext info`) to discover the module's API
/// surface without executing any functions.
world extension {
    import host;
    use host.{transaction};
    use types.{serialized-value, serialized-args, serialized-kind, serialized-arguments};

    /// Invoke a function exported by this module.
    ///
    /// `tx` is the capability for this call, lent by the host and revoked when
    /// the call returns. It is `none` when the host has no transaction to lend —
    /// for example `surreal ext run` outside a datastore — and a function
    /// that declared a transaction parameter then fails rather than proceeding
    /// without one. A function that declared none never touches it.
    ///
    /// A function that declared a read-only transaction cannot write through
    /// it. The host installs a no-write execution frame for the call, and a
    /// data-modifying statement is refused where it is reached, however deep.
    /// The declared mode is therefore enforced, not merely recorded.
    ///
    /// `name` identifies the function; pass `none` to call the module's default
    /// export (the unnamed function). Returns an error if the function does not
    /// exist or execution fails.
    export invoke: async func(tx: option<borrow<transaction>>, name: option<string>, args: serialized-args) -> result<serialized-value, string>;

    /// Return the names of all functions exported by this module.
    /// A `none` entry represents the default (unnamed) export.
    export list-functions: func() -> list<option<string>>;

    /// Return the named argument types for the given function.
    /// Used by the host for arity/type checking before invocation.
    export function-args: func(name: option<string>) -> result<serialized-arguments, string>;

    /// Return the declared return type for the given function.
    export function-returns: func(name: option<string>) -> result<serialized-kind, string>;

    /// Return whether the given function is marked as writeable.
    ///
    /// The guest declares whether a function needs write access; how that is
    /// spelled is the guest language's business.
    ///
    /// Consumed by planning and scheduling, and by the host to decide whether
    /// to install the no-write frame for the call. A function that reports
    /// `false` cannot write, whatever query string it builds.
    export function-writeable: func(name: option<string>) -> result<bool, string>;

    /// Return whether the given function declared a transaction parameter.
    ///
    /// A function that declared none is lent no handle at all: the host passes
    /// `none` for `tx`, so it has no route to the datastore whatever its body
    /// contains. Without this the host would have to lend every function a
    /// handle and trust the generated dispatch to drop it, which a hand-written
    /// or non-Rust component need not do.
    export function-declares-transaction: func(name: option<string>) -> result<bool, string>;

    /// Return the comment for the given function, if one was provided.
    /// Sourced from Rust doc comments or an explicit `#[surrealism(comment = "...")]`.
    /// Returns `none` when the function has no comment.
    export function-comment: func(name: option<string>) -> result<option<string>, string>;

    /// Optional initialization hook, run once at build time.
    ///
    /// `surreal ext build` runs it and ships the memory it leaves as the
    /// component's initial state, without this export. There it sees the
    /// attached files but no network, and must close every WASI resource it
    /// opens, or the build fails; an error it returns fails the build too.
    /// Anything it reads, including randomness and the clock, is frozen into
    /// the archive. The host refuses a component that still exports it.
    ///
    /// Lent no transaction, and synchronous by contract: its effects persist in
    /// linear memory for every later request, so it must not reach the
    /// datastore. A sync-lifted export also cannot block, so the
    /// `#[surrealism(init)]` macro rejects an `async fn`.
    export init: func() -> result<_, string>;
}
